(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-ans-adrenergic-quiz"]={"title":"Adrenergic Drugs Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What kind of alpha blockade does phenoxybenzamine produce?","choices":["Selective for alpha-one only","Selective for alpha-two only","Irreversible and noncompetitive","Competitive and reversible"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so recovery needs new receptor synthesis, taking at least a day. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Why is tyramine dangerous in a patient taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor?","choices":["Monoamine oxidase normally destroys it in the gut","It blocks reuptake of norepinephrine at the terminal","It is converted into amphetamine in the liver","It directly stimulates alpha-one receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is found in fermented foods such as cheese and wine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which receptors does oxymetazoline act on?","choices":["Alpha-one only","Dopaminergic","Alpha-one and alpha-two","Beta-one and beta-two"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, producing vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which receptor does phenylephrine act on, and what happens to the heart rate?","choices":["Alpha-two, with reduced sympathetic outflow","Alpha-one, with reflex bradycardia","Beta-one, with tachycardia","Beta-two, with no rate change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It has no direct cardiac action; the slowing is a baroreceptor reflex. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Norepinephrine extravasates from a drip and the skin blanches. What does the lecture give as treatment?","choices":["Phentolamine","Atropine","Dantrolene","Pralidoxime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An alpha blocker reverses the intense local vasoconstriction before the skin sloughs. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What does the lecture warn about stopping clonidine abruptly?","choices":["Adrenal suppression","Rebound nasal congestion","Rebound hypertension","Rebound bronchospasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is tapered rather than stopped. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which two enzymes inactivate the catecholamines, and where does each act?","choices":["Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft and pseudocholinesterase in plasma","Monoamine oxidase postsynaptically and catechol-O-methyltransferase in plasma","Cytochrome P450 in the liver alone","Catechol-O-methyltransferase postsynaptically and monoamine oxidase within the neuron"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and rapid inactivation by both is why catecholamines are short acting. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which hypertensive crisis does the lecture pair with phentolamine?","choices":["Crisis in thyroid storm","Abrupt clonidine withdrawal","Crisis in pre-eclampsia","Crisis from cocaine intoxication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is the rebound the clonidine slide warns about. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Why does cocaine exaggerate epinephrine's cardiovascular actions?","choices":["It converts epinephrine to norepinephrine","It prevents reuptake","It increases receptor synthesis","It inhibits monoamine oxidase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so the transmitter persists at the receptor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What does the lecture say happens when epinephrine is given to a patient on a beta blocker?","choices":["There is no clinically important interaction","Alpha effects are left unopposed","Both alpha and beta effects are abolished","The blood pressure falls sharply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the vasodilating beta side leaves only vasoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which adverse effects does the lecture list for dopamine?","choices":["Sedation and dry mouth","Bronchoconstriction","Nausea, hypertension and arrhythmias","Tremor, restlessness and anxiety"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What does the lecture warn happens if oxymetazoline is used beyond three days?","choices":["Nasal septal perforation","Rhinitis medicamentosa, or rebound congestion","Permanent loss of the sense of smell","Systemic hypertension requiring treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is the practical reason for a hard stop on a decongestant spray. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"How do epinephrine's effects change with dose?","choices":["Alpha effects predominate at low doses, beta at high doses","Only beta effects occur, at any dose","The effects are identical across the dose range","Beta effects predominate at low doses, alpha effects at high doses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so vasodilation gives way to vasoconstriction as the dose rises. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which metabolic effect does the lecture attribute to the alpha-one blockers used for hypertension?","choices":["Improved lipid profiles and glucose metabolism","Raised low-density lipoprotein and triglycerides","Fasting hypoglycaemia","No metabolic effect at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which contrasts with propranolol's worsening of both. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which non-cardiovascular use does the lecture give for phentolamine?","choices":["Treatment of impotence","Treatment of glaucoma","Treatment of asthma","Treatment of narcolepsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, injected to dilate penile arteries. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Why is pseudoephedrine kept behind the pharmacy counter?","choices":["It requires blood pressure monitoring","It was banned by the Food and Drug Administration","It can be converted to methamphetamine","It causes rebound congestion after three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is a regulatory rather than a pharmacological reason. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which two alpha-one blockers does the lecture direct to benign prostatic hyperplasia?","choices":["Phentolamine and phenoxybenzamine","Labetalol and carvedilol","Tamsulosin and alfuzosin","Prazosin and terazosin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while prazosin, terazosin and doxazosin are listed for hypertension. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What happened to ephedra-containing herbal products?","choices":["They remain freely available","They were banned in 2004","They were moved behind the pharmacy counter","They were reclassified as prescription only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Why does stopping propranolol abruptly risk arrhythmias?","choices":["Beta receptors have been permanently destroyed","Norepinephrine stores have been depleted","The drug accumulates and causes late toxicity","Beta receptors have been up-regulated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so the heart is left hypersensitive to catecholamines. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"How does reserpine act?","choices":["It blocks transmitter uptake into vesicles","It blocks release of stored norepinephrine from vesicles","It blocks reuptake of norepinephrine at the terminal","It inhibits monoamine oxidase within the neuron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, depleting norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What does the lecture say distinguishes dobutamine from other sympathomimetics?","choices":["It has no effect on atrioventricular conduction","It can be given orally","It produces no tolerance with prolonged use","It barely raises myocardial oxygen demand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which matters in a failing heart. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which receptors does isoproterenol predominantly stimulate?","choices":["Beta-one and beta-two","Alpha-one and alpha-two","Beta-one only","Alpha-one only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it heads the beta potency order and sits at the bottom of the alpha one. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Why does the lecture prefer a selective beta-two agonist for chronic asthma over epinephrine?","choices":["Longer duration of action","Greater potency at beta-two receptors","Oral availability","Absence of any tremor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine reverses acute bronchospasm; albuterol covers chronic treatment. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"How does an alpha-two agonist such as clonidine lower blood pressure?","choices":["It depletes vesicular norepinephrine","It reduces central sympathetic outflow","It blocks alpha-one receptors on vascular smooth muscle","It blocks beta-one receptors in the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so an agonist produces vasodilation by damping sympathetic drive. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"How is norepinephrine given, per the lecture?","choices":["As a single intramuscular injection","Orally, in divided doses","By inhalation","As a titrated intravenous infusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which suits a drug used to support vascular resistance in shock. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which of the two does the lecture link to preventing cardiovascular mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Carvedilol","Labetalol","Propranolol","Pindolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it also decreases lipid peroxidation and vascular wall thickening. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Through which receptor does amphetamine raise blood pressure?","choices":["Beta-two","Alpha-two","Dopaminergic","Alpha-one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with beta-one accounting for the cardiac stimulation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Why is propranolol hazardous for a patient prone to hypoglycaemia?","choices":["It increases glucose uptake into muscle","It blocks glucose absorption from the gut","It masks the response to hypoglycaemia","It raises insulin secretion directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it also decreases glycogenolysis and glucagon secretion. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Why are norepinephrine's actions diminished but not reversed by phenoxybenzamine?","choices":["Norepinephrine acts only on dopaminergic receptors","It lacks significant beta activity on vessels","Norepinephrine is not bound by alpha receptors","Norepinephrine is metabolised faster than epinephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so there is no vasodilating component left to unmask. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"How many steps does the lecture give for adrenergic neurotransmission?","choices":["Six","Three","Ten","Five"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, against the six sequential steps given for the cholinergic neuron. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 72"}]};
